+# Billing service
+
+`BillingService` (`src/service.py`) is the service layer for tenant-scoped
+billing data. It reads from the billing database (`src/repo.py`, an in-memory
+`FakeDB` in this repo) and caches some reads in an in-process cache
+(`src/cache.py`).
+
+Run the tests with `pytest`.
+
+## Caching
+
+In short: **billing data can be up to `ttl_seconds` old (5 minutes by default),
+but only if it was changed outside the service**, for example by a back-office
+tool editing the database directly. Changes made through `BillingService`
+show up on the next read.
+
+### Which reads are cached
+
+| Method                                   | Cached? |
+| ---------------------------------------- | ------- |
+| `get_customer(tenant, customer_id)`      | Yes     |
+| `get_invoice_summary(tenant, period)`    | Yes     |
+| `get_total_paid(tenant, customer_id)`    | Yes     |
+| `get_balance(tenant, customer_id)`       | No, always goes to the database |
+| `list_open_invoices(tenant, customer_id)`| No, always goes to the database |
+| Any read for the `internal` tenant       | No, always goes to the database |
+
+Empty results are cached too: an unknown customer (`None`) or a customer with
+nothing paid (`0`) is cached like any other value.
+
+Why some reads skip the cache:
+
+- **The `internal` tenant.** The audit service regenerates this data on every
+  request, and serving stale values is a compliance issue (SOX control C-14).
+  None of its reads are cached.
+- **`get_balance` and `list_open_invoices`.** These were taken out of the
+  cache after reports of one tenant seeing another tenant's data. The code
+  comment says the root cause was never found. Today's cache keys include the
+  tenant (see below), so these reads could probably be cached again safely,
+  but nobody has confirmed that. They are listed in `_UNCACHED_OPS` in
+  `src/service.py`.
+
+  Side effect: each call to either method also **clears the whole cache, for
+  all tenants**. This doesn't make data more stale. It does mean the next
+  cached reads go to the database more often.
+
+### What invalidates cached data
+
+- **Writes through the service.** `upsert_customer`, `create_invoice` and
+  `mark_invoice_paid` clear the **whole** cache, for every tenant, straight
+  after the database write. The next read of any cached method reloads from
+  the database, so data changed through the service is never stale.
+  Invalidation is all or nothing: there is no per-key or per-tenant
+  invalidation.
+- **Calls to `get_balance` / `list_open_invoices`.** These clear the whole
+  cache too (see above).
+- **Expiry (TTL).** Each entry records when it was loaded. It is served only
+  while it is younger than `ttl_seconds` (default `300`, set in the
+  `BillingService` constructor). Once it is that old, the next read drops it,
+  reloads from the database and starts a new TTL. An entry that is never read
+  again stays in memory, but it will never be served.
+
+### How stale can data be
+
+| How the data changed                                       | Maximum staleness |
+| ---------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------- |
+| Through the same `BillingService` (or one sharing its `Cache`) | None: the next read sees it |
+| Directly in the database, e.g. back-office tools (BILL-2324) | Up to `ttl_seconds` (default 5 minutes) |
+| Through a different `BillingService` with its own `Cache`   | Up to `ttl_seconds` |
+| `internal` tenant, `get_balance`, `list_open_invoices`      | None: always read from the database |
+
+The cache lives in process memory. Each `BillingService` creates its own
+`Cache` unless you pass one in. If several service instances or processes
+serve the same data, a write through one of them does **not** clear the
+others' caches, so those caches rely on the TTL.
+
+Setting `ttl_seconds=0` stops cached entries from ever being served, which
+effectively turns caching off. Negative values are rejected.
+
+For support: if a customer reports a wrong **balance** or **open-invoice
+list**, it is not caused by caching, because those reads always come from the
+database. A wrong **customer record, period summary or total paid** that fixes
+itself within about 5 minutes is most likely a cached value from before a
+direct database edit.
+
+### How tenants are kept apart
+
+The cache is a single store shared by all tenants. Every entry is keyed by the
+tuple `(tenant_id, operation, entity_id)`, built by `make_key` in
+`src/cache.py`. So:
+
+- the same `customer_id` or `period` in two tenants maps to two separate
+  entries;
+- different operations for the same entity never collide (for example,
+  `get_total_paid` and `get_customer` for `c-1`);
+- a tuple is used instead of a joined string such as `"acme:c-1"`, so IDs
+  containing `:` can't produce the same key for different tenants.
+
+Every database query the service makes is also filtered by `tenant_id`, so
+data loaded into the cache belongs to a single tenant.
